# Security

Security means protecting the app, users, files, and API keys.

A RAG app can contain sensitive company documents, so security matters a lot.

## Main Security Areas

```text
file upload safety
authentication
permissions
rate limits
secret management
CORS
input validation
logging without leaking private data
```

## File Upload Safety

The backend should check:

```text
Allowed file type
Maximum file size
Safe file name
No executable files
No unexpected MIME types
```

Example:

```text
Allow: PDF, TXT, Markdown
Block: EXE, ZIP, unknown binary files
```

## API Keys And Secrets

Never put real secrets in code.

Use `.env` locally and hosting environment variables in production.

Examples:

```env
GITHUB_TOKEN=secret
OPENAI_API_KEY=secret
DATABASE_URL=secret
```

Do not commit `.env` to GitHub.

## Permissions

The retriever should only search documents the user is allowed to access.

Example:

```text
Employee should not retrieve HR salary documents.
Manager should only retrieve branch documents they can access.
Admin can retrieve all documents.
```

## Rate Limits

Rate limits prevent abuse and high token costs.

Example:

```text
100 requests per 15 minutes per user
10 uploads per hour per user
```

## Input Validation

Validate user input.

Example:

```text
Question must not be empty.
Question length should have a limit.
Uploaded file must be allowed.
```

## CORS

CORS controls which frontend domains can call the backend.

Example:

```env
CORS_ORIGINS=https://your-frontend.com
```

Do not allow every domain in production unless there is a good reason.

## Logging Safety

Logs should help debugging, but they should not leak secrets.

Bad log:

```text
Authorization: Bearer real_api_token
```

Good log:

```text
chat request failed for user_001
```

## Simple Presentation Version

```text
Security protects documents, users, API keys, and model access. A production RAG app needs safe uploads, authentication, permissions, rate limits, secret management, validation, and careful logging.
```

